---
// B970 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// The page IS the recreation: the reference's signature layout fills the
// viewport edge-to-edge, exactly as the source frames itself. Every word the
// page owns lives in a single band on the top baseline, set across a twelve-
// column grid as 3/3/6 — identity stack, italic display title with its roman
// credit, and one paragraph closed by a Read more link. Everything below the
// band is artwork, run full bleed to all four edges. The mechanic is live where
// the source is live — Read more extends the essay in place; the band never
// moves. The film still is echoed, not pictured: the room inverts to an ink
// field with a tonal mass where the portrait sat, and the plate is credited
// with a mono specimen label. All spacing/type composes from kernel custom
// properties.
//
// Reference (internal, never shipped): refs/works/b970.jpg, 1600×1000.
// ref-geometry degenerates here (design.md §Known tool limits): the ground it
// detects (L=127) is the film still itself, not paper, so it collapses the
// frame into one 1000px "row" and reports the portrait as the "central block".
// Every number below was read off the jpg by eye and with per-pixel probes
// instead — thresholding for the white type (L>205) against the still, which
// separates the band cleanly where the tool's page-wide ground cannot.
//
// Archived case-study copy (removed from DOM 2026-07-17):
//
//   The signature —
//   The whole voice of the page is one band across the top: a wordmark with two
//   underlined links, an italic serif title over its roman credit, and a
//   four-line essay — three, three and six columns of a twelve-column grid, all
//   hung from the same baseline. The remaining ninety percent of the viewport
//   belongs to a full-bleed monochrome still, and the text never descends into
//   it. One Read more link is the only door the band offers.
//
//   Study notes —
//   The 3/3/6 split works because the columns widen with the weight of their
//   content — a two-line identity, a two-line caption, a full paragraph — so the
//   band reads left to right as an accelerating sentence. Pinning all three to
//   one baseline is what buys the full-bleed image its silence: the eye clears
//   the text in a single pass and drops into the still with nothing left to
//   negotiate. The serif display against semi-mono captions keeps the registers
//   legible even at one shared type size.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'b970');
const w = entry!.data;
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`} chrome="work">
  <main class="site">
    <div class="site__band">
      <header class="band__id">
        <span class="band__wordmark">{w.title} Archive</span>
        <span class="band__links" aria-hidden="true">
          <u>Contact</u>, <u>IG</u>
        </span>
      </header>

      <div class="band__caption">
        <em class="band__title">A Field in Three Columns</em>
        <span class="band__credit font-mono">{w.title}</span>
      </div>

      <div class="band__essay">
        <p>
          The page keeps its entire voice in this one strip: an identity, an italic title with
          its credit, and a single paragraph, divided three, three and six across twelve
          columns. Everything beneath the strip is given to the artwork, which runs full bleed
          to every edge of the viewport.
        </p>
        <p class="band__extra">
          The band itself never moves. Extending the essay pushes nothing but these lines; the
          field below absorbs the rest, and the two registers never compete.
        </p>
        <button class="band__more" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Read more</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <span class="site__plate font-mono ttu" aria-hidden="true">Plate 01 — 16 mm still</span>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  // Read more: extend the essay in place, band stays pinned. Live, like the source.
  const more = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.band__more');
  const extra = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.band__extra');
  more?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const open = extra?.toggleAttribute('data-open') ?? false;
    more.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
    more.textContent = open ? 'Read less' : 'Read more';
  });
</script>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room, now the whole page and inverted: the full-bleed still
     becomes an ink field, and every owned word sits in one top band. The page is
     its own query container — the stage used to provide one, so every cq* unit
     below would silently re-resolve against the viewport without this. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    background-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* The artwork IS the ground here, so Base's chrome fails twice over: its
     `← Index` is --ink on an --ink field (invisible in every mode, light and
     dark alike), and its reserved top-right corner is exactly where the essay
     column ends (measured ink to x=1566 of 1600, on the band's first line).
     Re-ink it to --paper and drop it to the opposite corner — the reference's
     lower field is unbroken still, and the specimen label holds the other side,
     so it lands on empty field and stays in a corner, which is what the sitewide
     rule is actually protecting. */
  :global(.chrome-back) {
    top: auto;
    bottom: max(var(--chrome-inset), env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  /* Tonal echo of the portrait: a lighter mass low-centre, under a faint
     scan texture — the artwork's weight without the artwork. */
  .site::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        0deg,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--paper) 4%, transparent) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px 4px
      ),
      radial-gradient(
        58% 72% at 54% 64%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--paper) 18%, transparent),
        transparent 72%
      );
  }

  /* Transcribed from the reference at 1600×1000. The 3/3/6 grid is measured, not
     assumed: ink starts x29 / x417 / x808 and the essay's longest line ends
     x1566, which solves to a 12-track grid spanning x29→1568 at a 129.8px pitch
     — so column-gap 1.2% (18.6px), not the stage-era 1.5%, puts columns 4 and 7
     back within a pixel of their measured starts.

     One type size serves the whole band: cap top y34 → baseline y46 is capH=13px
     in every column, and Archivo's cap is 0.686em (read from the woff2), so
     13/0.686 = 18.95px = 1.184cqw at 1600. The old 0.95rem cap was sized for the
     ~1000px stage and ran 1.25× small; the old `vw` also ignored the container.
     Baselines step 46 → 70 → 94 → 118, so leading is 24px = 1.267. `lh-copy`
     (1.5) is gone from the essay for exactly that reason — it fought the
     measurement. With that leading Archivo's baseline sits 18.3px below the box
     top, so a 2.77% top puts the first baseline back on y=46. */
  .site__band {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 2.77% 2% auto 1.8%;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    column-gap: 1.2%;
    font-size: clamp(0.8rem, 1.184cqw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.267;
    z-index: 1;
  }
  .band__id {
    grid-column: 1 / 4;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  /* Measured: baseline y70, underline y71→73. */
  .band__links u {
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }

  .band__caption {
    grid-column: 4 / 7;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  /* The pairing the source trades on: italic display over a semi-mono roman
     credit, at the shared band size. */
  .band__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }
  /* The source sets this credit in the same serif roman as the band; our kernel
     answers that register shift with mono, so match it on cap height, not glyph
     width. IBM Plex Mono's cap is 0.698em against Archivo's 0.686 (both read
     from the woff2), so 0.686/0.698 = 0.983em lands its caps on the band's
     13px exactly. */
  .band__credit {
    font-size: 0.983em;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }

  .band__essay {
    grid-column: 7 / 13;
  }
  .band__essay p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .band__extra {
    display: none;
  }
  /* Read more sits one blank line under the essay — measured, its baseline is
     y167 against the last essay line's y118, i.e. two 24px steps. 1.3em of the
     band size is that step, so the expansion stays on the band's baseline grid
     instead of the stage-era 1em. */
  .band__extra[data-open] {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1.3em;
  }
  .band__more {
    margin-top: 1.3em;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  /* --spot resolves to --ink in the essential and generative kernels, which on
     this inverted sheet would draw an ink ring on an ink field. --paper is the
     only value that survives every mode here. */
  .band__more:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--border-w, 2px) solid var(--paper);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* Specimen credit for the echoed plate — mono, bottom margin, quiet. */
  .site__plate {
    position: absolute;
    left: 1.8%;
    bottom: 4%;
    font-size: var(--type-meta, 0.65rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--paper) 55%, transparent);
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* Phone: the source layout adapting, not a second design. The band is still
     one strip hung from the top baseline and the field still takes everything
     below it — but three columns of a twelve-column grid cannot hold a wordmark
     at this width, so the 3/3/6 sentence runs top-to-bottom instead of left-to-
     right, keeping its reading order and its one shared type size. */
  @container (max-width: 40rem) {
    .site__band {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      row-gap: 1.3em;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }
    .band__id,
    .band__caption,
    .band__essay {
      grid-column: 1;
    }
  }
</style>
